    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d15">

                <pb facs="0023" n="11" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg11"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860D.6359 International Nickel Company/24:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-21T17:00:00+03:00">February 21,
                            1941—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 22—3:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>331. With reference to Helsinki’s No. 37 of February 7, 3 p.m., regarding the
                    Petsamo nickel mines, the Swedish Minister told me today that he had discussed
                    the matter yesterday with the Finnish Minister<anchor n="30" xml:id="d15-f1"/>
                    who, while stating that the present status of the negotiations is as outlined to
                    Schoenfeld by the Finnish Minister for Foreign Affairs, expressed some pessimism
                    as to a prompt settlement.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d15-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d15-f1" n="30" xml:id="d15-f1-content">
                <p>Juho Kusti Paasikivi.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d15-f2" n="31" xml:id="d15-f2-content">
                <p>On February 18, 1941, the Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs of the
                    Soviet Union, Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, told the Finnish Minister that
                    there was no reason to continue with the work of the Mixed Committee in Moscow
                    and that the matter would now take its course, with all its consequences. See
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Finland Reveals Her Secret Documents on Soviet
                        Policy, March 1940–June 1941</hi>, pp. 27, 94.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
